WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.400
Batch tuning without measurement quickly becomes guesswork.

00:00:03.400 --> 00:00:06.155
Start with a repeatable benchmark that reflects the real workload.

00:00:06.155 --> 00:00:10.120
Use consistent prompts, fixed context lengths, the same model and quantization,

00:00:10.120 --> 00:00:11.497
and a quiet machine.

00:00:11.497 --> 00:00:14.605
Record the runtime version and hardware state so you can explain results later.

00:00:14.605 --> 00:00:15.900
A quick test can be useful,

00:00:15.900 --> 00:00:19.050
but a repeatable test is what lets you compare settings honestly.

00:00:19.050 --> 00:00:20.340
Measure more than one number.

00:00:20.340 --> 00:00:24.745
Prompt processing speed tells you how quickly the runtime handles existing context.

00:00:24.745 --> 00:00:26.980
Generation speed, often reported as tokens per second,

00:00:26.980 --> 00:00:29.338
tells you how quickly new tokens appear.

00:00:29.338 --> 00:00:31.875
First-token latency tells you how responsive the system feels.

00:00:31.875 --> 00:00:35.120
Total response time shows what the user experiences end to end.

00:00:35.120 --> 00:00:38.200
For services, also track requests per second and queue time.

00:00:38.200 --> 00:00:40.520
Watch resource behavior while the benchmark runs.

00:00:40.520 --> 00:00:44.450
GPU utilization, CPU utilization, VRAM use, system RAM, temperatures,

00:00:44.450 --> 00:00:47.340
and throttling can explain why a setting improved or failed.

00:00:47.340 --> 00:00:51.300
A result that looks fast during a cool one-minute test may degrade after the hardware

00:00:51.300 --> 00:00:52.930
heats up.

00:00:52.930 --> 00:00:56.880
Background tasks, driver activity, caching, and warmup effects can also distort results.

00:00:56.880 --> 00:00:58.600
Change one setting at a time whenever possible.

00:00:58.600 --> 00:01:02.815
If you increase batch size, change context length, and raise concurrency in the same test,

00:01:02.815 --> 00:01:05.210
you may get a number but you will not know what caused it.

00:01:05.210 --> 00:01:08.215
Keep a simple table of settings, metrics, and observations.

00:01:08.215 --> 00:01:11.890
The discipline of measurement is what turns tuning from folklore into an engineering

00:01:11.890 --> 00:01:12.480
process.

00:01:12.480 --> 00:01:16.270
For team use, write down the benchmark as if someone else will repeat it next month.

00:01:16.270 --> 00:01:19.970
Include the exact prompt shape, generation limit, runtime version, model file,

00:01:19.970 --> 00:01:23.400
quantization, hardware, driver version, and the settings being compared.

00:01:23.400 --> 00:01:24.910
The notes do not need to be elaborate,

00:01:24.910 --> 00:01:28.795
but they should be specific enough to prevent a future tuning conversation from becoming

00:01:28.795 --> 00:01:29.460
guesswork again.
